	</a>
	
		<figcaption>Book Number Nineteen (burgundy-coloured). Cover: The Master Sword in Hama beads.</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<p>After <a href="/post/2021/07/book-number-fourteen/">Book Number Fourteen</a>, I already anticipated running out of Hama beads I use to personalise the cover. The next book, Number Twenty, is truly the last one where I can just paste on a pre-made figure (that I made in batch about fifteen years ago). So to quote myself:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I might have to go out and buy more Hama beads.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This time that statement is true.</p>
<hr>
<p>In an attempt to make these landmark posts slightly more interesting to the average reader, here&rsquo;s an overview of tips that is not guaranteed to be completely up to update:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="/post/2023/03/creating-journals-that-last/">Create journals that last</a> or <a href="/post/2022/03/expiry-dates-on-journals/">burn them after they&rsquo;re full</a>&mdash;up to you.</li>
<li><a href="/post/2021/11/from-analog-notebook-to-digital-vault/">Digitise your notes</a>. I simply use GeniusScan on the phone, export a huge <code>.pdf</code>, and run that through a script that spits out a <code>.md</code> file linking the converted <code>.jpg</code> of each page. I then have to write keywords by hand to make the whole thing searchable.</li>
<li><a href="/post/2021/09/hp-sprocket-mini-printer/">Print small photos</a> and stick them in there.</li>
<li><a href="/post/2025/05/writing-is-taking-a-stab-at-immortality/">Writing is taking a stab at immortality</a> but only if you decide to publish. Let the notebook be the start of it all.</li>
<li><a href="/post/2017/07/journaling-in-practice/">Journaling is good for the soul</a>. This post includes practical hints I compiled in 2017.</li>
</ul>
<p>Be wild. <em>Think Different</em>. Oh wait, that slogan has already been taken. You know what I mean. I once smeared some molten chocolate in there and squeezed out a blueberry on it. I stuck a spiced chai teabag on there. I bought a French stamp that smells like baguettes for too much money just to put in my own private notebook. Trigger all five senses! Or six if you&rsquo;re Bruce Willis! This sounds like more blog post material.</p>
<p>Book Number One started in August 2010. Exciting to see when Number Twenty will pop &lsquo;round that will be a true cause for celebration.</p>


          
